## 2. Why Traditional API Security Cannot See Agentic Failure Modes

Standard API gateways, WAFs, and service-to-service OAuth tokens were designed for deterministic callers. When an AI agent sits behind those credentials, four new failure classes bypass traditional perimeter controls:

{% cardgrid title="Four Failure Classes That Bypass Traditional API Gateways" label="Failure Mode" %}
- **1. Indirect Prompt Injection.** Untrusted data from CSVs, emails, or RAG chunks shares the same attention window as system instructions. An attacker does not need to breach the network; they only need the agent to read a poisoned record.
- **2. Confused-Deputy Parameter Smuggling.** The API gateway sees a valid backend `client_credentials` token from the agent service. It has no idea that the LLM filled `employeeId: "emp-0001"` because a regular user asked it to override the parameter in chat.
- **3. Timeout Mutation Amplification.** When a downstream POST takes 10.1 seconds and returns `504 Gateway Timeout`, a naive ReAct loop treats the timeout as a failed call and re-invokes the tool—creating duplicate financial or operational transactions.
- **4. Live MCP Tool Drift.** External Model Context Protocol (`MCP`) servers can emit `notifications/tools/list_changed` at runtime, silently mutating tool descriptions or adding hidden exfiltration parameters after initial review.
{% /cardgrid %}

---

## 3. Defense-in-Depth Across Five Explicit Trust Boundaries

Because a probabilistic planner can always be coerced into *proposing* a bad action, a governed architecture assumes the reasoning runtime will eventually be tricked—and designs **five physical and cryptographic trust boundaries (`Boundary A` through `Boundary E`)** so a compromised proposal can never become an unauthorized execution.

